Throughout this module, I explored several Windows security features and learned how they help protect a computer against malware, unauthorized access, and data loss.
I studied Windows Update and Windows Security, understanding the importance of keeping the operating system and its security definitions up to date.
I also explored Virus & Threat Protection, learning about different scanning options, threat history, quarantine, real-time protection, and ransomware protection.

Another important part of this module was learning how Windows Firewall controls incoming and outgoing network traffic. I explored the three network profiles: Domain, 
Private, and Public, understanding how each one applies to different network environments and why firewall protection is important.

I also studied Device Security, including Core Isolation, Memory Integrity, and the Trusted Platform Module (TPM), which provides hardware-based security features.
Additionally, I learned how BitLocker uses encryption to protect data and how a Startup Key can be used on systems without a compatible TPM.

Finally, I explored the Volume Shadow Copy Service (VSS), 
understanding how Windows creates snapshots of data to support recovery and how these copies can be targeted by ransomware attacks.

This module helped me develop a better understanding of Windows security mechanisms, including threat protection, network security, encryption, and data recovery. 
These concepts are important in Cybersecurity, especially when analyzing system security, identifying potential vulnerabilities,
and understanding how to protect Windows environments.
